  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe key insight is to exploit sparsity in the solution by treating the overlap integral as a max operation over discrete shifts. We address the non-convexity by reformulating the constraint and using a sparse representation. The central idea is to search for solutions that are zero-valued at most positions, containing only a few significant \"bumps\". This reduces the high-dimensional optimization to a lower-dimensional combinatorial search. We maintain a tent function structure and adjust it to minimize the maximum correlation with its shifted versions. The average improvement we aim for is a reduction of at least 0.001 relative to the current record. This approach avoids getting trapped in shallow local minima and leverages the observation that near-optimal solutions tend to be structurally simple with few non-zero components.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Variables: A row-sparse binary matrix M \u2208 {0,1}^{n\u00d7N} (where N is the number of possible shifts), h \u2208 \u211d^n (tent function samples), and constraints on the sum metrics.\n- Objective Function: C_max = max_{k=1}^{N-n} |\u27e8h, 1-h_\u00b7k\u27e9| (discrete correlation)\n- Constraints:\n  - \u2211_{i\u2208S} h[i] = n/2 for a support set S of size s (the number of non-zero components)\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- Key Formulation Details: We use the discrete correlation normalized by dx, but treat the max operation as a component-wise constraint. The essential mathematics translates to minimizing the maximum overlap coefficient across all shifts while maintaining integral control and tent function smoothness at the optimization nodes.\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Parent Construction and Validation:\n   - If parent exists, validate the solution:\n     - Ensure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n     - Check sum(h) = n/2 exactly (using floating-point tolerance)\n     - Calculate C5_valid = compute_c5(h, n)\n   - If parent is unavailable, construct a candidate using:\n     - Tent function based on 12 equally spaced peaks\n     - Apply support selection to reduce the number of active peaks\n\n2. Algorithmic Initialization:\n   - Generation of Candidate Solutions:\n     - Determine optimal s (non-zero mass) via binary search on s from ceil(sqrt(n/3)) to ceil(sqrt(n))\n     - Initialize peak positions and heights using a tent function model\n     - Use the parent as initial if available\n\n3. Core Search Loop:\n   - Shift Detection and Pruning:\n     - Use combinatorial search to detect significant shifts (k with high correlation)\n     - Monitor progress: elapsed time, improvement rate, and solution validity\n   - Tent Function Adjustment:\n     - For each candidate, form a tent function using the peaks\n     - Optimize peak heights with floating-sum constraints\n   - Local Refinement:\n     - Apply projected gradient descent for local improvement\n     - Use line search with adaptive step sizes\n\n4. Search Varieties & Parameter Tuning:\n   - Primary Search: Structured search over possible shift patterns (order from few to many peaks)\n   - Secondary Optimization: Gradient-based refinement for found candidates\n   - Dynamic Parameter Adaptation:\n     - \u03b1 (smoothness) reduces by 0.9 when stagnation detected\n     - Population size doubles when no improvement occurs\n   - Individual Response Steps are handled by the core search loop with:\n     - Detect shifts \u2192 generate candidates \u2192 validate and refine\n\n5. Search Condition Details:\n   - Termination when:\n     - Elapsed time exceeds budget\n     - No improvement in 10 consecutive iterations\n     - Local minima detected (gradient/divergence indicators)\n   - In response to detected condition, we scale down the search space considerably and switch to alternate exploration strategy\n\n6. Validation and Finalization:\n   - Final score calculation: weighted correlation across all shifts\n   - Minimal validation: check sum against n/2 \u00b1 tolerance_bound\n   - Output format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) with h[i] guaranteed to [0,1] via project_feasible",
